Version the method and understand what a snapshot contains (@rc-versioning: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @reproducible-computation, #source "../sources/Pro-Git.lingua#r_7NQ43JGJ863RMR0HDHA7GMRD9G", #source "../sources/Research-Software-Engineering.lingua#r_07D4PDD4HGKNK3PW4M90YSV2QY", #reproducible-computation "Collaboration-and-review.lingua#r_0ARV56N560SEN2PCT7XRDT2XZK") { r_3SQ3GK6XAMY85MDN8RCZC6DVXM
**Read:** [Pro Git — Chacon and Straub](../sources/Pro-Git.lingua#r_7NQ43JGJ863RMR0HDHA7GMRD9G), chapters 1–3 and 5, then the relevant chapter 7 tools and chapter 10; [Research Software Engineering with Python — Irving and colleagues](../sources/Research-Software-Engineering.lingua#r_07D4PDD4HGKNK3PW4M90YSV2QY), the version-control chapters in sequence.

Use a personal exercise repository to practice Git; the trail is not an instruction to alter the history of another project's working branch. Begin with the distinction between working files, staged content, and a committed snapshot. Inspect a diff before committing so the recorded method corresponds to the files you intended to preserve.

A commit identifies recorded repository content and ancestry. It does not automatically include untracked files, ignored observations, installed libraries, remote services, large external data, or a dirty working-tree modification used during a run. Record those dependencies or their resolvable identities separately. A branch name or mutable tag is a location in history, while a specific commit names a particular revision under the repository's object model.

Keep source, small original test inputs, tests, and methodological documentation under appropriate versioning. Large or restricted observations may need another authorized store, with versioned metadata and content identities linking them to the analysis. Do not commit downloaded books or external datasets merely to make a research directory appear self-contained.

**Work:** make a first commit of your own teaching script and input, change a formula, inspect the diff, and commit an explained correction. Reconstruct the earlier version in a separate personal exercise copy and show how its result differs. Add an untracked parameter file and explain why the commit identity alone no longer specifies the run. Inspect the stored objects in Pro Git's later chapter until the distinction between an object identifier and a filename is clear.

Read chapters 1–3 and 5 completely; use the Portuguese translation alongside the English text if useful. Return to chapter 7 for advanced operations and chapter 10 for internals. A version history helps explain changes only when its messages and associated scientific account identify why the method changed.

**Continue:** [Review scientific changes and resolve conflicts with meaning intact](Collaboration-and-review.lingua#r_0ARV56N560SEN2PCT7XRDT2XZK).

